  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 313 PM EDT Sat Oct 17 2026\n\nNot a cloud in the sky this afternoon as the UP is sandwiched \nbetween expansive high pressure that extends from the lower Midwest \ndown through the southern Plains and deepening low pressure east of \nEdmonton, Canada. The tight pressure gradient draped across the \nUpper Midwest and a 35-45 kt 850 mb LLJ has created elevated SW wind \ngusts between 20-30 mph with a few gusts between 30-40 mph in the \nKeweenaw. Very dry air advection and efficient mixing into a dry \nairmass aloft has allowed RHs across the UP to drop into the 20s and \n30s with a few locations managing to plummet as low as 15-20%. \nDespite the near critical fire wx winds and RH, temps have only \npeaked in the mid 50s to near 60. Plus, widespread rain over the \nlast 48 hours have helped moisten short time-lagged fuels as well as \ncurtail fire spread on the Rugged Terrain Complex. So, there may be \na limited fire weather risk but high to extreme fire danger is not \nanticipated. A similar setup presents itself tomorrow as a sharp \ntrough and aforementioned Canadian low pressure works across \nnorthern Ontario, bringing another day of WAA and gusty SW winds. \n850 mb temps near 14-16C will allow temps to rise a bit warmer \ntomorrow, reaching the low 60s. Stronger mid-level flow between 40-\n50kts should kick up winds between 25-35 mph UP wide. Latest NBM \nguidance suggests 50-90% chance for gusts >40 mph in the Keweenaw \nand downsloping locations of the western and central UP by the early \nafternoon. Area soundings suggest similar mixing into a dry airmass \naloft, so if RHs manage to fall as low as they have this afternoon, \na more elevated fire weather risk may present itself.\n\nWinds will lighten Sunday evening, and continue to remain light \nthrough at least Wednesday morning thanks to another strong surface \nhigh migrating across the northern Plains toward eastern North \nAmerica. Airmass aloft will stabilize, with 850mb temps generally \nremaining between +5 and 10C, varying by model. This should yield \ndaytime highs peaking in the mid 60s for most interior locations and \ncloser to 60F lakeside, and maybe upper 50s in the Keweenaw. \nOvernight lows most nights in the 30s should be expected inland, \nwith 40s by the lakeshores. Next best shot of rain, albeit \nprobabilities are currently low, comes late Wednesday into Thursday.",
